# gmktec-xubuntu.recorded-facts — the second pool node's RECORDED facts, so the
# profile beside this file cannot silently disagree with the host.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS, and why for THIS node in particular. `gmktec-xubuntu.env` is
# the DATA the rebuild procedure consumes; this file is the RECORD that data
# must agree with. The first node earned its record by drifting from the host
# unnoticed (see `poweredge-xubuntu.recorded-facts`). This node earned its own
# before it was ever built: four of its values were written from the FIRST
# node's profile as placeholders, marked `# UNVERIFIED`, and the 2026-09-06 read
# found ALL FOUR wrong — two labels that do not exist, a boot entry whose case
# did not match, and an operator account with no passwd entry on this host.
# Every one of them read as a perfectly ordinary profile line. The marker is
# gone now, so the thing that keeps them honest is this table and the assertion
# that compares them.
#
# PROVENANCE. Read on the live host `gmktec-xubuntu` on 2026-09-06,
# READ-ONLY, with `lsblk -o NAME,LABEL,FSTYPE,PARTLABEL /dev/nvme0n1`,
# `sudo efibootmgr`, `getent passwd` and `id`; the partition sizes and
# mountpoints are the same day's `lsblk` and the stage-1 dry run against the
# node. THE DIRECTION OF TRUST IS ONE WAY: a value here changes only by
# re-reading the host, never by copying what the profile happens to say. A
# disagreement is a defect in the profile until the record says otherwise.
#
# SCOPE. Only what this node's profile CONSUMES: the volumes and tiers the
# procedure creates, the two partitions it preserves, and the four base-OS keys
# the read resolved. The node's hardware facts at large belong to the per-host
# information repository and not to this tree
# (SPECIFICATION/non-functional-requirements.md §"Runner-pool node rebuild
# recipe", "Facts in the host record, the procedure here").
#
# FORMAT. `#` comments and blank lines are ignored; every other line is a
# whitespace-separated record whose first field names its kind:
#
#   vg    <volume-group> <physical-volume-device>
#   lv    <volume-group> <logical-volume> <size> <fstype> <label>
#   tier  <role-label> <volume-group>
#   part  <device> <fstype> <size> <mountpoint>
#   key   <profile-key> [<value>]
#
# A `key` record with NO value states that the key is EMPTY, which is the
# format's spelling for "this filesystem carries no label" (`../profile.sh`
# §"How this format spells 'no label'") and is exactly how the profile spells
# it. It is a recorded fact in its own right, not an omission: "no label" is
# what the read found, and the assertion fails just as loudly if the profile
# ever grows one.

# What the procedure CREATES. One volume group, because this node has one
# medium: the unpartitioned tail of its single NVMe drive, which stage 1 takes
# as partition 3. Kernel path and not `/dev/disk/by-id/` on purpose — one drive,
# and it is the device the node already boots from, so there is no second
# spelling for either name to disambiguate.
vg nvmea /dev/nvme0n1p3
lv nvmea ci-cache 350GiB ext4 ci-cache
lv nvmea ci-containerd 525GiB ext4 ci-containerd
lv nvmea ci-workvols 525GiB xfs ci-workvols

# Role tiers — all three on the one group. The first node spreads its three
# across three groups; that is a fact about that node's hardware and not a rule
# of the procedure, which is why the placement is data.
tier ci-cache nvmea
tier ci-containerd nvmea
tier ci-workvols nvmea

# What the node ALREADY carries and the procedure PRESERVES. These two are why
# `DISK_PLAN=free-space`, and they are the reason stage 1 derives partition 3
# rather than assuming it: the table already numbers 1 and 2, so 3 is the next
# free number, and it must be the one `VOLUME_GROUPS` spells out above.
part /dev/nvme0n1p1 ext4 465.7G /
part /dev/nvme0n1p2 vfat 1G /boot/efi

# The four values the 2026-09-06 read resolved, each of which the profile had
# wrong. The two empty ones are the record's whole point: `ESP` and `root` are
# labels this host does not have, and nothing but a table like this notices that
# a plausible label is a fiction.
key ESP_LABEL
key ROOT_LABEL
key BOOT_ENTRY_LABEL Ubuntu
key OPERATOR_ACCOUNT cwoolley
